From bb1b8029b9cc3767fac5d2e90790134db4ce0955 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 15 Jun 2026 09:40:02 -0700
Subject: [PATCH RFC v3 0/4] net: flow_dissector: opt-in fast-path for common eth+IP+TCP/UDP shapes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

__skb_flow_dissect() walks every packet with the generic protocol-graph
parser. This adds an opt-in, straight-line fast-path for the common
eth + IP + TCP/UDP shape (IPv4 and IPv6), gated by a static_branch and
default off. Output is byte-identical to the slow path.

What it does
  - Fast-path for eth + IPv{4,6} + {TCP,UDP}. Anything else (IPv4
    options/frags, IPv6 ext headers, other shapes) falls through to the
    existing parser unchanged.
  - Per-shape gates under /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/ (eth_ip is the
    base; vlan/qinq stack on it). All default off.
  - Cost when off: one not-taken branch per dissect.

Safety
  - Byte-identical by construction: the fast-path either writes the same
    flow keys as the slow path, or returns false and defers to it -
    there is no third outcome. Verified against the slow path per shape
    (v4, v6, vlan, qinq).

Numbers - dissector cost in isolation (userspace A/B, same source built
two ways, eth+IPv4+TCP, ns/pkt, lower is better):

    x86 Zen2 (TR 3945WX)      12.4 -> 6.6    -47%
    x86 Skylake (i9-10885H)   10.6 -> 5.6    -47%
    ARM Cortex-A53           119.0 -> 61.3   -48%
    ARM Cortex-A72            42.4 -> 19.1   -55%
    ARM Cortex-A76            19.4 -> 9.3    -52%
    RISC-V SpacemiT X60       98.2 -> 49.3   -50%

  - IPv6 uses the same gate, also byte-identical (x86 -39%, RISC-V -25%).
  - It is ~half the retired instructions, so it holds across ISAs;
    largest on narrow in-order cores. (Zen 1 is the exception: ~0%,
    masked on that uarch.)

Where it runs in practice
  - skb_get_hash() callers: RPS/RFS, fq / fq_codel / cake, tc-flower.
  - Example: a WireGuard gateway with cake - __skb_flow_dissect fired
    ~1.1M times in 10 s on a 902 Mbit/s flow (cake classifying the
    encrypted UDP transport, which is the eth_ip shape).

Limitations (honest)
  - This is a per-packet CPU saving. On a link- or queue-bound host it
    appears as CPU headroom, not throughput; our macro testbed deltas
    are within run-to-run noise.
  - Absolute ns/pkt are per-ISA baselines; the cross-arch ratios are the
    point.

Patches
  1  eth + IPv{4,6} + {TCP,UDP}  (eth_ip)      byte-identical
  2  + single VLAN                              byte-identical
  3  + QinQ (depth-2)                           byte-identical
  4  VXLAN inner descent                        RFC EXPERIMENT (not b-i,
                                                gated separately)

  Base: net-next c0aa5f13826d. Supersedes v1 (in net-next as
  eeca3eb493b8) and v2 (8013aee91ccb); both withdrawn.

Backup detail (microbench harness, full per-shape x per-uarch matrix,
per-uarch perf counters, WireGuard bpftrace, byte-identical checks):
https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2  (perf-results/2026-06-25-*)

Open question for the list: given the byte-identical guarantee and that
eth+IP+TCP/UDP is the common case, is there interest in eth_ip defaulting
to on? Shipping default-off here to stay conservative.

